'''Cynthia Ann Brewer''' (born 1960) is an American cartographer, author, and [[professor]] of [[geography]] at [[Pennsylvania State University]]. Brewer's specialty relates to visibility and [[color theory]] in [[cartography]]. In 2023, she was awarded the [[International Cartographic Association]]'s highest honor, the [[International_Cartographic_Association#Carl_Mannerfelt_Gold_Medal|Carl Mannerfelt Gold Medal]], for her distinguished contribution to the field. 

Her web, print, and [[colorblind]]-friendly sets of colors, known as the Brewer palettes, have been used by numerous projects.<ref name="Gardner">Stephen D. Gardner, 2005, [http://www.personal.psu.edu/cab38/ColorBrewer/Steve_Gardner_thesis_PSU.pdf Evaluation of the ColorBrewer Color Schemes for Accommodation of Map Readers with Impaired Color Vision] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180729071809/http://www.personal.psu.edu/cab38/ColorBrewer/Steve_Gardner_thesis_PSU.pdf |date=2018-07-29 }} (6.1MB PDF)</ref><ref name="Emily2022">{{cite web |last1=Zaccardi |first1=Emily |title=Down from the Shelf: Accommodating Color Palettes for All |url=https://www.aag.org/down-from-the-shelf-accommodating-color-palettes-for-all/ |website=Stories |date=3 January 2022 |publisher=American Association of Geographers |access-date=2 February 2024}}</ref> She is the creator of the online color palette tool [[ColorBrewer]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=ColorBrewer 2.0 |url=https://colorbrewer2.org/# |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220630195444/https://colorbrewer2.org/#type=sequential&scheme=BuGn&n=3 |archive-date=June 30, 2022 |access-date=July 6, 2022}}</ref>

Brewer has worked as a map and atlas design consultant for the [[U.S. Census Bureau]], [[National Cancer Institute]], [[National Center for Health Statistics]], and [[National Park Service]]. She teaches courses in introductory cartography and map design.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Brewer|first1=Cynthia|title=Designing Better Maps: A Guide for GIS Users|publisher=Esri Press|language=en|date=30 July 2005|id= {{ASIN|1589480899|country=uk}}}}</ref> She also works on [[topographic map]] design, multi-scale mapping, generalization, and atlas mapping. She has been influential as a theorist for map representations and [[Geographic Information System|GIS]] professionals.

==Education==
She graduated from [[McMaster University]] (Ontario, Canada) in 1979 and the [[University of Guelph]] (Ontario, Canada) in 1983.<ref name="Roth2010">{{cite journal |last1=Roth |first1=Robert E. |title=Interview with a Celebrity Cartographer: Cindy Brewer |journal=Cartographic Perspectives |date=2010 |volume=66 |issue=66 |pages=91–101 |doi=10.14714/CP66.104 |url=https://cartographicperspectives.org/index.php/journal/article/view/cp66-roth/pdf |access-date=2 February 2024|doi-access=free }}</ref> She did her master's degree in geography with emphasis in cartography at [[Michigan State University]], 1983 to 1986, presenting a thesis titled ''The Development of Process-Printed [[Munsell chart|Munsell Chart]]s for Selecting Map Colors''. After a year at [[University of California, Santa Barbara|University of California at Santa Barbara]], she obtained her doctorate from Michigan State University in 1991. Her dissertation was ''Prediction of Surround-Induced Changes in Map Color Appearance''.<ref name="Penn State">{{cite web|title=Brewer, Cynthia {{!}} Penn State Department of Geography|url=http://www.geog.psu.edu/people/brewer-cynthia|website=www.geog.psu.edu|publisher=Penn State|access-date=26 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170320092251/http://www.geog.psu.edu/people/brewer-cynthia|archive-date=20 March 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Academic career==
She was visiting lecturer at the [[University of California at Santa Barbara]], Department of Geography during the year 1986/87.
On completing her doctorate she was assistant professor, for three years (1991 to 1994) at [[San Diego State University]]. She joined the [[Pennsylvania State University]], Department of Geography in 1994 and has been professor since 2007 and was head of the department from 2014 to 2021.

She has been a faculty member of the Center of Excellence for Geospatial Information Science (CEGIS), U.S. Geological Survey, Department of Interior, since 2008.<ref name="Penn State"/>

==ColorBrewer==
[[File:ColorBrewer-screenshot.png|thumb|[[ColorBrewer]] screenshot]]
{{Main|ColorBrewer}}

[[ColorBrewer]] is an online tool developed in 2002 for selecting [[thematic map]] color schemes based on Brewer's palettes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Miller |first1=Greg |title=The Cartographer Who's Transforming Map Design |url=https://www.wired.com/2014/10/cindy-brewer-map-design/ |magazine=Wired |access-date=31 January 2024}}</ref> The ColorBrewer palettes have uses beyond cartography; in 2018 climatologist [[Ed Hawkins (climatologist)|Ed Hawkins]] chose ColorBrewer reds and blues for [[warming stripes]] graphics portraying [[climate change|global warming]].<ref name=Voila_20191203>{{cite web |author-last=Bugden |author-first=Erica |title=Do you really understand the influential warming stripes? |url=https://www.chezvoila.com/blog/warmingstripes |website=Voilà Information Design |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191205172628/https://www.chezvoila.com/blog/warmingstripes |archive-date=5 December 2019 |date=3 December 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

